e  or that  somehow incorporate that track into the  theme  um i know that at the time cupcakes and  coasters position on the whole matter  was that it wouldn't be necessary and it  doesn't really matter  but personally i just think it would be  really cool like a new  creative avenue for the roller coasters  imagineering uh to see how the design of  the roller coasters themselves could be  featured  into a design of a whole themed area  which i think is not too ridiculous of  an idea i think there's one roller  coaster type which does this in real  life which is the mine train coaster  which obviously it's themed after a mine  train but not just that the track  itself looks like you know train rails  of course it's a little bit different  but i think it's the case where the  track of the roller coaster itself is  the most  immersive and there are still a lot of  hidden parts to it as well  i know that disneyland for instance  tries to hide the locations of their  block breaks as much as possible  i highly recommend checking out el toro  ryan's  newest video on black what big thunder  mountain which is  a really cool you know in-depth look  into  exactly how big thunder mountain works  that kind of reveals all of the behind  the scenes stuff and reveals just how  much behind the scenes there is there's  still a lot that's hidden with these  kinds of coasters  but the parts that you do see of the  coaster and that you really just  have to see because it's what you're  riding and um  it's it's all the mechanical stuff that  has to be visible all of that is very  much  themed and so for me at least it's a  ride type that's more  immersive than other coaster types  because when you're  when you're flying with a b m flying  coaster through  some kind of rocky terrain like in in a  happy valley park in china  you have these beautiful environments  and just awesome  settings but you can still see all of  the b m track around you the steel  supports that  create this machine and you know it's  it's something that you can't really get  around very easily but it still breaks  the immersion a little bit  compared to a mine train where you can  really imagine that you're on this wild  train  ride and all of the track that you see  is roller coaster track but it's also  part of this  immersive idea that you're on a wild  train ride  so i think it's really cool and  an idea that could be played with more  now the challenge is of course  what kind of coasters can you actually  incorporate into certain themes  at the time my idea was that in a  steampunk theme for instance and  honestly i think steampunk is the kind  of theme where  you can go you can go the most crazy  with roller coaster tracks and make it a  believable kind of contraption within  the narrative  of the of the themed area um but  i was basically thinking of some kind of  suspended transportation system  a little bit inspired by the city in  bioshock  or by something that my friend jazz made  in city skylines recently where he had  some floating cities  and suspended monorails in between them  as a transport method  suspended coasters could really fulfill  this kind of steampunk  you know futuristic travel  machinery role which is of course a  little bit ridiculous  but within that whole suspense of  disbelief i think it's  very much a setting where the coaster  design itself can be part of the theming  so for this i'm not going quite that far  and really building a floating steampunk  city but  in line with the steampunk theme that  digital dan has created for his area  just before me and next next to me on  the map  as well as the mind theme that was  established at the start of this map  i am kind of going for this so  my idea for this and yeah just  just hold on to my uh to my thoughts  because i'm gonna  go a little bit off topic on this i  guess but my idea was  uh cable cars are a transport  technology that's very much linked to  mines as well  in fact arguably the first cable car  that ever existed which was  in gdansk in poland around the 17th  century i believe  was actually built for a very similar  function it was built to transport  soil to some part of the city that was  being expanded  and while at the time this cable car  used ropes  and horses to power the whole thing  which makes it a lot less efficient than  today's cable cars  it's the first time that massive amounts  of rope  were hauled onto supports and dragged  material to one place  from one place to another and it's still  something that happens with a lot of  mines  actually uh doppelmeyer still creates  ropeway technology to transport goods in  mines  and excavation sites and some other  companies do as well it's  it's something that's still very  relevant and  yeah i figured it would fit the the mind  theme  that this this whole area is very  vaguely alluding to  and also in line with that i would have  to say that i'm starting to work  on the station here and stuff is kind of  going slow because the menus aren't  really being as friendly as i'd like  them to be and there's a lot of testing  you know what kind of pieces  i am using so this is some stuff that  i'm really gonna have to upgrade my pc  for because this is  not gonna be very doable in the long run  um but yeah i'm just  gonna deal with it here um but i was  really inspired by a particular mine  in belgium which is called winterslach  which has a beautiful hallway with  ironworks supports holding up this  triangular roof  and lots of brickwork and decorations on  the side  um so very vaguely inspired by that i'm  building the station building here  really not going too much into detail  here um keeping everything quite  simple um in part because  well i wanted to finish this whole  venture  in a timely manner um but still try to  use as much of the kind of  flemish revival aesthetic  that i could see which is not per se  the aesthetic of the mine it's a little  bit more fancy than  um mine buildings were although there  are some very  fancy mine buildings um but i just  really like flemish revival it's a  really cool  kind of renaissance revival style that  you find all throughout belgium  and you find some very similar styles in  the netherlands as well  features lots of intricate masonry and  cornerstones and i just love it  lots of different colors um it's it's a  style that was really popular  around the end of the 19th century and i  know in the netherlands a similar style  was led by pierre calpers and led to  buildings like the national museum  at the reich museum and also the  amsterdam central station which are very  similar buildings but you can really see  these sorts of elements of the style in  there as well  and of course what helps is that the  winter slug building  as a as an industrial mine building is  very open space  that has no supports in the middle which  kind of makes it perfect for a coaster  station i'm really just taking  all of my ideas from there here and  creating a similar kind of  big iron garden girder structure to hold  up the roof  and create this large space underneath  it where the station can sit  especially because suspended coasters do  need quite a bit of space for this  station as well  with the only caveats that the station  is a bit too long  at the time when i was building the  station i still had eight cars  on the train i later brought this down  to five cars  partly because eight cars felt too heavy  for the support structure  even though i'm not an engineer and the  support structure is probably not that  on point anyway  um i felt that eight cars really would  be asking too much  of the suspended track here but also i  simply didn't have space for a larger  transfer table  at the end of the ride so something had  to give i didn't want the transfer table  to realistically be able to actually  transfer trains in and out so that i  could run two trains  so i'm going with two trains of five  cars instead  which in any case is much better than a  single train of eight cars  but yeah it's probably not the very best  situation  for throughput anyway but yeah  keeping this station very simple and not  really adding too much to it  it's just a rectangle with two entrances  uh one on the front one on the back with  the the exit path actually being on the  back which is  maybe not the most practical solution  because that is where the most  amount of space is to create a cue path  i just always like to have my entrance  on the front because it very clearly  demarcates where the entrance is at and  then also have the  actual you know path into the the queue  somewhere in that area as well so that  it's kind of clear where you have to go  if you want to enter this coaster  and the front is a little bit more  detailed than the back  but there's not too much to it some  simple spires but i'm not going  overboard here  and creating my own spires like i  sometimes like to do as well  now skipping forward a little bit and  yes  i made a bunch of cuts in these time  lapses just to make everything  a little bit more sped up because i'm  trying to fit it all into a single video  um but yeah going forward a little bit i  am working on a small building just to  house some of the  the horrible cattle pens that are going  on in this queue here  please ignore what a terrible cue this  is normally i'm always a man of very  themed cues and  i hate boring cues honestly i think it's  something that  theme parks in general should put more  attention towards  i mean you really are spending most of  your time in a theme park especially on  a busy day  standing in queues or walking from one  attraction to the next one  so put some effort into it but yeah i'm  going against my own mentors here and  just making this a very simple  switchback building  uh with a kind of industrial route those  sawtooth kind of roofs  not too much going on it's very simple  but i think in the layout of this whole  area it just kind of works  and i had to fit that cue in somewhere  might as well do it over here because  well i couldn't really come up with  anything that would be much better  in a perfect world i would love to do  like  more of a barren 1898 queue with maybe  even  a bigger connected building uh going  through  some kind of garden beforehand but  i didn't really have the time to try any  of those  more advanced ideas so i'm keeping this  one very simple  and just trying to go as much as i can  with the kind of  industrial slash mind theme that was  established beforehand  um so yeah not too much going on here so  let me actually  skip forward to something else that was  happening later on  um i decided to build a little lake over  here  and well i think it's something that  at least gives you some interesting  shots because at this point i was really  just  building in a way that i figured would  look cool for  the final cinematic footage don't hate  me for this please  it's just something that you gotta do  sometimes i was just really thinking  about how the coaster would look off  right and figured  hey a pond over here might be really  cool also added this lake  on the other side of the layout which is  really to get some good  camera angles honestly i think it's fine  sometimes to build in ways that you know  you're already picking some camera  angles  and thinking of them and trying to build  around that as well it's something that  i do a lot  it's something that i don't really admit  to or talk about a lot  but still i often build in a way that i  think would work out for a video  and you know it's not something that can  be really bad  as long as you're trying to stick to  some realistic layouts as well  but sometimes it's good to try and set  up some shots because otherwise  you know you build your things and then  it's just a complete  uh surprise what it's going to look like  and especially from the vantage points  of parts  you should always i think check it out  as much as you can  while you're building stuff see what it  looks like and see how it all comes  together  and based on that either keep going with  it or try to change the composition  of it a little bit uh just so you're not  really surprised  about what the coaster looks like once  you're actually down there  and looking from an actual perspective  at it  rather than just the aerial perspective  which you're normally  looking at it from when you're actually  building it  now for all of the foliage around here  i'm really keeping it very simple adding  some  very basic rock work and some bushes  here and there  um but nothing too out of the ordinary i  would say  in the middle i couldn't really think of  what i wanted to do specifically  but i just knew that i wanted to keep  this space very open because  i like the fact that you know on the  suspended sections of trek  you're spending a lot of time in the air  without any supports  and especially if you have any parts you  know directly underneath that you get  that interesting interaction where  from the coaster you're flying over  these parts which is cool and when  you're standing on the paths you can see  the coaster directly above you  i i'm not sure if this is enough  distance  uh of a clearance to like require  coaster nets  i decided not to install any coaster  nets because i figured it was high  enough  uh up there which kind of doesn't make  sense because if any objects do fall out  they're just gonna  go faster but then again  how do you stop that at these heights  but yeah i just didn't want to  actually add any coaster nets because  they would totally ruin the look of this  whole area and the openness of the space  not to mention that i do think that  these are very difficult cars to throw  things  out of in real life of course you always  want to make sure that when a coaster  comes close to paths you want to make  sure that  any kind of loose items falling out  don't accidentally hit on somebody's  head because if you get hit with a phone  in your head that travels at 100  kilometers an hour  it's probably gonna be very lethal or  super damaging at the very least  um but with these kinds of very enclosed  cars that the suspended coaster has  i think it's fine i'm not even entirely  sure why i'm going on about this so much  people probably wouldn't have noticed if  i hadn't talked about it  um but yeah it still irks me a little  bit that there aren't any coaster nets  i just really wanted the space between  these giant  pillars to remain as free as possible to  really give you that feeling  how the the coasters are flying over  these suspended tracks  from basically every vantage point  and then for all of the foliage around  it i'm also trying to mix  a little bit with the foliage that the  surrounding  areas uh have so  just taking some trees from some of  their stuff  and filling it in with some oaks this  honestly  is very similar to how i would always  use the fill in the maps  on coaster college  which i i also try to do the very fast  and easy way just creating these tree  lines by sinking a bunch of trees into  the ground  and covering it up with different  species of trees as well  there's really not too much to it but i  think especially from a distance it  works well enough to make it feel as  though  you actually are somewhere in a forest  and in a real life location  rather than just a random sandbox  in planet coaster i do think it's  interesting though that  we're on this you know red sand western  style map but everybody decided to  change the color of the ground to grass  maybe it's just because  that red sand just doesn't really work  for most themes except for western  but it's something that was just kind of  striking  and finally for this final stretch i'm  just embellishing the area with some  extra scenery  even though it's not really themed i  still wanted to add some fountains and  some basic gardening and everything  but really the the focus of this whole  build  is just on the coaster itself so  not going to end it in detail here just  trying to make sure that no area stays  untouched  and doesn't like totally bring you out  of the immersion of the whole area  but there's something that i do want to  show before i  move on to the cinematic footage and the  pov  which is one other thing that i actually  built  beyond just the simple gardens which is  a flat ride i eventually ended up  choosing to build a troika here  because i think it works out the best to  actually preserve the view of this  and i'll be totally honest i built this  whole thing because i think it would  look cool  there's not really any other purpose to  this flat ride other than just  working in the general layout of this  area  and providing a really cool shot but  honestly i think it worked out in the  end  and it's just something that i wanted to  feature because all of the other areas  in the park do have at least multiple  rides  or you know some kind of flat ride focus  as well which of course i'll just say as  a reminder you can find in the playlist  as well  but really for my purpose all of the  focus is on the coaster itself and this  is just something that  helps provide a little something extra  and  one cool shots that i had in mind  actually for the video  um also i just noticed again one of  these like spikes  for anybody who is you know really  concerned about this  and about the comments about it i know i  need to fix up my computer but at this  point  it's more of a time thing than it is a  money thing because  i am able to afford an upgrade i just  really need to  like find the time to get into it and at  the moment my whole life is a bit  uncertain and kind of wonky and  i really don't have the time to look  into this kind of thing  another problem is that planet coaster  is the only game which is especially  problematic  with planet 2 of course koali zoo is a  lost cause  but um park attack still runs okay and i  can  still play city skylines so i don't know  what's up with planet coaster but it's  just running especially terribly  and eventually i only got it to work  decently  through nvidia geforce but  whatever the case it's something that  you shouldn't be too  um concerned about i think it'll be fine  that sounds totally like something  somebody would say who knows that  things aren't fine but no by that i mean  i'm gonna look into it at some point in  the future but right now  there are too many other things that i  need to worry about  anyway that's not something that i want  to bore you with  let's get down to the the final section  of this video  um so i can actually show you the  cinematic footage and the pov  so after building this little  fountain thingy here it's finally time  to  show you what the right looks like so  with that all said i hope you enjoyed  this video  and um i'll see you in the next video  whatever that may be  [Music]  so  [Music]  you


